// BlackWhiteFrontDoor.swift: the first thing "Black & White.app" runs.
//
// This port ships with no game content at all. The first time it is opened it asks for the
// player's own copy of Black & White (a disc image, a folder that is a copy of the disc, or
// the folder the game is installed in, of any version), checks it file by file against
// game-manifest.json, builds the game folder from it — the program files are generated by
// the app itself, so no copy of the game is refused for carrying a different program — and
// from then on starts the game at once. What the player chose is only read, never changed.
//
//   swiftc -O -parse-as-library -target arm64-apple-macos13.0 BlackWhiteFrontDoor.swift
//
// build-app.py does that and puts the result in the app as Contents/MacOS/BlackWhite. The
// shell launcher it hands over to is Resources/BlackWhiteLauncher.sh (launcher.sh).
//
// Without a window, for tests:
//   --selftest            checks the parsing, hashing and setup detection, one line each
//   --check-setup         prints where the game folder, disc copy and program are, and
//                         whether set-up is complete (exit 0) or not (exit 1)
//   --import-iso <path>   sets the game up from a disc image, printing each step
//   --import-disc <path>  the same from a folder that is a copy of the disc
//   --import-folder <path> the same from an installed copy; a Creature Isle image,
//                         disc folder or installed copy adds the expansion instead
//   --setup               show the set-up window even when the game is set up
//                         (as holding the Option key while opening the app does)
//
// BW_APP_SUPPORT_DIR and BW_APP_LOGS_DIR move the player's folders, as they do for
// launcher.sh; BW_UNSHIELD names an unshield other than the one bundled in the app.

